digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56871-1
  rankdir="TB";
  "I_BG1" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Currently there are fluctuating conditions causing stress, and Adaptation is important for survival, so Stress adaptation is crucial for bacteria."];
  "I_BG2" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: B. subtilis has evolved stress survival ways, and GSR is a prominent way, so B. subtilis uses GSR for stress survival."];
  "I_Conc1" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Currently None critical for sensing, and If WT data exists for comparison then Sensing capability retained, so RsbR N-term not critical for sensing."];
  "I_Conc2" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Currently Reexamination prompted, and If Deletion retains response then Stressosome not required, so Stressosome not essential for sensing."];
  "I_Exp1" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Study sought evidence, and Reasoned N-terminal important, so Experimental Plan: Engineer N-terminal substitutions."];
  "I_Exp2" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Region 11-58 substituted, and Strain lacks rsbR/rsbP, so Experimental Strain isolates RsbRA N-terminal function."];
  "I_Gap1" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Currently Substitutions did not impact sensitivity, and If Kinase stimulation not necessary then Sensing model is questioned, so Evidence contradicts RsbR sensing model."];
  "I_Gap2" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Currently Evidence is elusive, and If Fig 1 tests sensing region then New test is needed, so Need new experimental evidence for sensing."];
  "I_Gap3" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Currently Evidence contradicts model, and If Need new evidence then Hypothesis must be tested, so Goal: Test RsbR-mediated stress sensing."];
  "I_Hyp1" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Currently RsbR proteins are proposed as sensors, and If orientation is consistent with sensing then hypothesis is supported, so Hypothesis: RsbR serves as sensor."];
  "I_Hyp2" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: YtvA/RsbR orthologs sense signals, and RsbR paralogs impact responses, so RsbR proteins likely have sensory function."];
  "I_Mech1" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Sigma B activates regulon in response to stress, and Energy stress is a type of stress, so Sigma B responds to energy stress."];
  "I_Mech4" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-reasoning: Currently sigma B is inactive by RsbW, and If RsbVW controls activity then RsbW inhibits, so RsbW inhibits sigma B in the switch."];
  "I_Mech7" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-reasoning: Currently RsbP/U dephosphorylate RsbV, and If Energy activates RsbP and Env activates RsbU then separate pathways exist, so Separate pathways control RsbV dephosphorylation."];
  "I_Mod1" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Currently Stressosome is thought to sense stress, and If RsbT release is central then Stressosome controls RsbT, so Model: Stressosome controls RsbT release."];
  "I_Mod2" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Currently RsbX reverses levels, and If it is not clear how stress is sensed then mechanism is unknown, so Model exists but sensing mechanism is unclear."];
  "I_Mod3" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Currently Model says Stressosome controls RsbT, and If sensing mechanism is unclear then Model is incomplete, so Existing Model is incomplete regarding sensing."];
  "I_Res1" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-reasoning: Currently Strain has reporter, and If Predictions exist for mutants then Outcomes can be measured, so Predicted Outcomes defined for assay."];
  "I_Res2" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Activity measured upon ethanol, and Some subs increased activity, so Substitutions affect response magnitude."];
  "I_Res3" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Some subs decreased activity, and Some subs unchanged, so Substitutions have varied effects on magnitude."];
  "I_Str1" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Stressosome is a complex of RsbR/S, and RsbR/S have STAS domains, so Stressosome core is formed by STAS domains."];
  "I_Str2" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: RsbR has N-terminal globin dimers, and There are multiple RsbR paralogs, so Stressosome has diverse sensory extensions."];
  "I_Str3" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Currently Stressosome has sensory extensions, and If sensory extensions exist then sensing function is likely, so Stressosome likely has a sensing function."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study investigates whether environmental stress is sensed primarily through kinase RsbT to activate sigma B independently of RsbR/S-containing stressosome complexes, and evaluates whether the stressosome chiefly modulates the strength and timing of the general stress response rather than serving as the initial sensor.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Evidence that stressosome components affect sigma B output but are not strictly required for environmental stress-inducible RsbT-dependent signaling motivates a revised sensing model."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Stressosomes regulate the availability of RsbT that can activate RsbU, so they can modulate RsbU activation through RsbT sequestration."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Within the known RsbP/RsbU branches controlling RsbV dephosphorylation, stressosome control of RsbT availability places the stressosome upstream of RsbU activation in the environmental-stress branch model."];
  "R21" [label="(0,0,0) Prior models and paralog evidence make RsbR-mediated stress sensing a plausible working hypothesis to test, rather than an established mechanism."];
  "R27" [label="(0,0,0) Engineering RsbRA N-terminal substitutions in a strain lacking rsbR genes and rsbP provides an experimental design to test RsbRA N-terminal function in stress sensing."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Because stress adaptation is crucial and B. subtilis uses the sigma B-regulated GSR as a prominent stress-survival mechanism, the GSR is an important stress-adaptation system in B. subtilis without proving it is strictly required for survival."];
  "R31" [label="(0,0,0) The substitutions have varied effects on sigma B response magnitude, with some increasing, some decreasing, and some not changing peak activity."];
  "R32" [label="(0,0,0) The reporter assay results show that RsbRA N-terminal substitutions can affect sigma B output magnitude."];
  "R35" [label="(0,0,0) The data indicate that the tested RsbRA N-terminal region is not critical for sensing and that cells lacking stressosomes can still retain an RsbT-dependent environmental stress-inducible sigma B response."];
  "R36" [label="(0,0,0) Because stressosome loss does not abolish the RsbT-dependent environmental stress-inducible sigma B response, the model should be refined so the stressosome is not treated as the sole environmental stress sensor."];
  "R37" [label="(0,0,0) The GSR and sigma B framework provides a stress-response context for studying how B. subtilis handles energy and environmental stress inputs."];
  "R38" [label="(0,0,0) A defined part of the sigma B GSR mechanism is the RsbV/RsbW partner switch involving RsbV phosphorylation state."];
  "R39" [label="(0,0,0) The stressosome is plausibly connected to the environmental-stress branch of the GSR through its regulation of RsbT availability and its proposed sensing-related structure."];
  "R40" [label="(0,0,0) Because the stressosome is connected to the RsbT/RsbU branch but the sensing mechanism remains unclear, the existing environmental stress-sensing model requires refinement."];
  "R41" [label="(0,0,0) The plausible RsbR-sensing hypothesis addresses the unresolved model gap and motivates direct experimental testing."];
  "R42" [label="(0,0,0) The model gap and the need for new evidence motivate an investigation plan to test RsbR-mediated stress sensing."];
  "R43" [label="(0,0,0) The investigation plan and RsbRA substitution design provide a concrete experimental approach for testing the RsbR-mediated sensing hypothesis."];
  "R44" [label="(0,0,0) The experimental approach yielded evidence that RsbRA N-terminal substitutions alter sigma B output magnitude."];
  "R45" [label="(0,0,0) Together, the substitution evidence and model-refining data support a revised view in which RsbT can mediate stressosome-independent environmental sensing."];
  "R46" [label="(0,0,0) The refined model and supporting data motivate the scientific idea that RsbT can mediate stressosome-independent environmental sensing, with the stressosome not acting as the sole sensor."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Environmental and physical stressors such as salt, ethanol, heat, and acid are relevant stress inputs for the sigma B-linked general stress response context."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Sigma B is implicated in responses to energy stress and to environmental or physical stress contexts relevant to the GSR."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Upon stress exposure, RsbV becomes dephosphorylated and a preferred partner for RsbW, linking stress to the RsbV/RsbW partner-switching control of sigma B."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) The sigma B partner-switch involves RsbW inhibition and stress-dependent changes in RsbV phosphorylation or binding state."];
  "S1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently there are fluctuating conditions in nature causing cellular stress."];
  "S10" [label="(10,0,0) In unstressed cells, sigma B is held inactive by the anti-sigma factor RsbW."];
  "S11" [label="(11,0,0) RsbW is the kinase of RsbV, the anti-anti-sigma factor of sigma B."];
  "S12" [label="(12,0,0) Upon stress exposure, RsbV becomes dephosphorylated, making it a preferred partner for RsbW."];
  "S13" [label="(13,0,0) RsbV is dephosphorylated by two known serine/threonine phosphatases, RsbP and RsbU."];
  "S14" [label="(14,0,0) Energy stress activates RsbP, whereas environmental stress activates RsbU."];
  "S15" [label="(15,0,0) RsbU phosphatase activity is activated by interaction with RsbT."];
  "S16" [label="(16,0,0) The availability of RsbT to activate RsbU is controlled by sequestration of RsbT in stressosomes."];
  "S17" [label="(17,0,0) Fig 1 shows impact of Ala substitutions in putative sensing region of RsbRA."];
  "S2" [label="(2,0,0) Appropriate sensing and adaptation to stressors is important for cell survival."];
  "S22" [label="(22,0,0) Schematic shows region of residues from 11 to 58 subjected to Ala substitutions."];
  "S23" [label="(23,0,0) Mutant rsbRA genes were added back to a strain background lacking rsbR genes and rsbP."];
  "S24" [label="(24,0,0) The strain carried a sigma B-responsive reporter to assess the general stress response."];
  "S25" [label="(25,0,0) Nonfunctional RsbRA predicted to yield constitutive response, sensing-dead unable to activate."];
  "S26" [label="(26,0,0) The black line shows wild-type data mean of biological triplicate experiments."];
  "S27" [label="(27,0,0) Graphs show beta-galactosidase activity as proxy for sigma B activity upon ethanol stimulation."];
  "S3" [label="(3,0,0) Bacillus subtilis has evolved ways to sense and survive environmental stresses."];
  "S32" [label="(32,0,0) Some substitutions resulted in increased peak sigma B activity."];
  "S33" [label="(33,0,0) Some substitutions resulted in decreased peak sigma B activity."];
  "S34" [label="(34,0,0) Some substitutions resulted in unchanged peak sigma B activity."];
  "S39" [label="(39,0,0) Each stressosome is a 1.8-MDa protein complex comprising RsbR dimers and RsbS."];
  "S4" [label="(4,0,0) The general stress response (GSR) is a prominent way regulated by sigma B."];
  "S40" [label="(40,0,0) RsbR and RsbS proteins both include a STAS domain forming the core structure."];
  "S41" [label="(41,0,0) RsbR proteins bear less-conserved N-terminal non-heme globin dimers."];
  "S42" [label="(42,0,0) B. subtilis encodes five intact RsbR paralogs and a blue light sensor YtvA."];
  "S43" [label="(43,0,0) The stressosome is thought to sense environmental stress and activate RsbT kinase activity."];
  "S44" [label="(44,0,0) In this model, the release of RsbT is the central event in the environmental stress sensing pathway."];
  "S45" [label="(45,0,0) Elevated sigma B levels are reversed by phosphatase RsbX which dephosphorylates RsbS."];
  "S46" [label="(46,0,0) Despite structural information, it is not clear how stress is sensed by the stressosome."];
  "S47" [label="(47,0,0) One attractive model is that RsbR proteins serve as sensors and activators of RsbT."];
  "S48" [label="(48,0,0) The outward orientation of RsbR globin dimers is consistent with a sensing function."];
  "S49" [label="(49,0,0) YtvA senses blue light and heme-binding RsbR orthologs sense oxygen."];
  "S5" [label="(5,0,0) In B. subtilis, sigma B activates the general stress regulon in response to stress."];
  "S50" [label="(50,0,0) RsbR paralogs impact sigma B responses consistent with a sensory function."];
  "S51" [label="(51,0,0) Substitutions in the linker impacted steady-state output but not stress sensitivity."];
  "S52" [label="(52,0,0) Stimulation of RsbT kinase activity is not necessary for sigma B activation."];
  "S53" [label="(53,0,0) Experimental evidence for stress sensing by RsbRs has remained elusive."];
  "S54" [label="(54,0,0) Here, we sought experimental evidence for RsbR-mediated stress sensing using RsbRA as a model."];
  "S55" [label="(55,0,0) We reasoned N-terminal regions might include residues important for sensing and engineered substitutions."];
  "S56" [label="(56,0,0) Substitutions influenced magnitude and duration but none was critical for stress sensing."];
  "S57" [label="(57,0,0) Results prompted reexamination of the requirement for the stressosome in environmental stress sensing."];
  "S58" [label="(58,0,0) Cells deleted for stressosomes retained an environmental stress-inducible sigma B response dependent on RsbT."];
  "S59" [label="(59,0,0) Data refine model showing RsbT can mediate stressosome-independent environmental sensing."];
  "S6" [label="(6,0,0) Energy stress includes starvation and treatments limiting biological processes."];
  "S7" [label="(7,0,0) Environmental and physical stressors include salt, ethanol, heat, and acid."];
  "S9" [label="(9,0,0) In B. subtilis, sigma B activity is controlled by the RsbVW partner-switching mechanism."];
